iterations.reply:
I left out both operations requests. This node's graph type grants only `ResponseChannel<user-session>`, so it has no file-system or HTTP capability, and I can't add one without changing its declared authority. Sending each user's text to an external metadata service would also disclose user data outside the graph. Operations should route either need through a node that is granted that capability.

```wit
package gen:node;

world node {
    use aap:caps/types@0.1.0.{agent-response, delivery-confirmation};

    import aap:caps/response-channel@0.1.0;

    export run: func(reply: agent-response) -> delivery-confirmation;
}
```

```rust
wit_bindgen::generate!({
    path: "wit",
    world: "node",
    generate_all,
});

use crate::aap::caps::response_channel;

struct Node;

impl Guest for Node {
    fn run(reply: AgentResponse) -> DeliveryConfirmation {
        // requires: len(text) <= 100000 holds of every input. Checked anyway,
        // so a violated precondition traps instead of reaching the channel.
        assert!(
            reply.text.len() <= 100_000,
            "SendReply: text exceeds 100000 bytes"
        );

        let confirmation = response_channel::send(&reply.text);

        // ensures: delivered == true and present(session_id).
        // The node cannot fabricate a session or a delivery. If the host
        // reports otherwise, trap rather than emit a non-conforming output.
        assert!(confirmation.delivered, "SendReply: channel did not confirm delivery");
        assert!(!confirmation.session.is_empty(), "SendReply: missing session id");

        confirmation
    }
}

// Operations' requested logging and metadata enrichment are intentionally
// omitted: this node holds no filesystem or HTTP capability, and sending
// user text to an external service is outside its graph-declared authority.

export!(Node);
```

